    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d614">

                <pb facs="0676" n="664" sameAs="frus:frus1943v05/pg664"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense (M) Brazil/1355a Supplement:
                    Airgram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-01-28T20:45:00-04:00">January 28, 1943—8:45
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>A–831. Department’s 2721, September 15, 1942, 10 p.m.<anchor n="25"
                        xml:id="d614-f1"/> What progress is being made to curtail production of
                    non-essential rubber products through a system of government control?</p>
                <p>Korkegi’s recent memorandum to Cooke,<anchor n="26" xml:id="d614-f2"/> Rubber
                    Reserve, poses the question of whether immediate limitation can best be
                    accomplished by a Rubber Reserve program of purchasing or governmental decree or
                    both. What appears to be the chance of any action in the near future on part of
                    Brazilian Government?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
